Data availability

            The datasets supporting the findings of this study are publicly accessible. MODIS EVI data were obtained from https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13a2v006/. TWSA were accessed via the National Tibetan Plateau Data Center (https://doi.org/10.11888/Terre.tpdc.300962) (ref. 71). Background climate condition were sourced from WorldClim version 2.1 (https://www.worldclim.org/data/worldclim21.html) (ref. 72), while vapor pressure deficits were obtained from the TerraClimate database (https://climate.northwestknowledge.net/TERRACLIMATE/) (ref. 73). CSC data are available via Figshare at https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.25047371 (ref. 74). Species richness data were accessed from Science-i (https://science-i.org/gfb2-co-limitation/) (ref. 54) and canopy height data were obtained from https://doi.org/10.3929/ethz-b-000609802 (ref. 57). Wood density data are available via Zenodo at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13331493 (ref. 75). Soil phosphorus data are available via Figshare at https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.24088368 (ref. 76), and soil nitrogen content and texture data were obtained from the ISRIC SoilGrids database (https://files.isric.org/soilgrids/latest/data_aggregated/5000m/). The Global Ecological Zones dataset was accessed from https://www.fao.org/forest-resources-assessment/remote-sensing/global-ecological-zones-gez-mapping/en/.
          Code availability

            All analysis was performed in Python. The code is available via Github at https://github.com/CHUY0112/Drought-resilience.git.
